    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "av98nxo",
      "content": "<strong>CHAPTER 1 — DC CIRCUITS</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3vcbt4h",
      "content": "Direct Current (DC) circuits are the foundation of all electrical engineering. A DC circuit carries current in only one direction. Understanding DC circuit laws and theorems is essential not only for circuit analysis but also as a basis for understanding AC circuits and electrical machines. This chapter covers Ohm's Law, Kirchhoff's Laws, methods of circuit analysis, and the most important network theorems."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "uazbmah",
      "content": "<strong>1.1  Ohm's Law</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gttfvyh",
      "content": "Ohm's Law states that the voltage across a conductor is directly proportional to the current flowing through it, provided the physical conditions (temperature, material) remain constant."
    },
    {
      "id": "3jk072j",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>V = I × R        where V = Voltage (Volts), I = Current (Amperes), R = Resistance (Ohms Ω)</strong></p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pokzuwl",
      "content": "Rearranging: I = V/R and R = V/I. Ohm's Law applies only to linear, bilateral elements. Non-linear elements like diodes and transistors do not obey Ohm's Law."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "j2bm8y9",
      "content": "<strong>Power Relationships</strong>"
    },
    {
      "id": "73j6x7o",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>P = VI = I²R = V²/R        (Power dissipated in a resistor)</strong></p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "bblos76",
      "type": "table",
