            "<p>NTC — thermistor applications</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Germanium</p>",
            "<p>Negative</p>",
            "<p>NTC semiconductor</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "f967j9o",
      "content": "<strong>1.2  Series and Parallel Resistor Combinations</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "e2vwu2n",
      "content": "<strong>Series Connection</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ponb4ry",
      "content": "In a series circuit, the same current flows through all components. The total resistance is the sum of individual resistances."
    },
    {
      "id": "n95zeil",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>R_total = R₁ + R₂ + R₃ + … + Rn</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>V_total = V₁ + V₂ + V₃       (Voltage Divider)</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>V_k = V_total × (R_k / R_total)       (Voltage across k-th resistor)</strong></p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "79m3u0t",
      "content": "<strong>Parallel Connection</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kf127wi",
      "content": "In a parallel circuit, the same voltage appears across all branches. The total current is the sum of branch currents."
    },
    {
      "id": "1i8et0w",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>1/R_total = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + 1/R₃ + …</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>For two resistors: R_total = (R₁ × R₂) / (R₁ + R₂)</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>I_k = I_total × (R_total / R_k)       (Current Divider Rule)</strong></p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "g7a6s6x",
      "content": "<strong>1.3  Kirchhoff's Laws</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "87tg9be",
      "content": "Kirchhoff's Laws are fundamental to circuit analysis. They apply to any network, regardless of the types of elements (linear or non-linear, active or passive)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "k4r3ch0",
      "content": "<strong>Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL)</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8wx70bg",
      "content": "The algebraic sum of all currents entering and leaving any node (junction) in a circuit is zero. This is based on the principle of conservation of electric charge."
    },
    {
      "id": "3872z7x",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>ΣI_entering = ΣI_leaving        OR        ΣI = 0 at a node</strong></p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1z28aya",
      "content": "Example: At a node, if three currents enter (I₁ = 3A, I₂ = 2A) and two currents leave (I₃ = ? and I₄ = 1A): By KCL: 3 + 2 = I₃ + 1, therefore I₃ = 4A."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "vw5fohr",
      "content": "<strong>Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL)</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8r5g8ad",
      "content": "The algebraic sum of all voltages around any closed loop in a circuit is zero. This is based on the principle of conservation of energy."
    },
    {
      "id": "e7ub4a0",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>ΣV = 0 around any closed loop</strong></p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dlfq2jc",
      "content": "Sign Convention: While traversing a loop, if you pass through a resistor in the direction of assumed current, voltage drop is negative. If you pass through a voltage source from − to +, it is a voltage rise (positive)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "krgaafc",
      "content": "<strong>Mesh Analysis (Loop Current Method)</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jyblhod",
      "content": "Mesh Analysis applies KVL to each independent loop (mesh) in a planar circuit. Assign a mesh current to each mesh (usually clockwise). Write KVL for each mesh. Solve the simultaneous equations to find mesh currents. Branch currents are then calculated from mesh currents."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xmt5nwd",
      "content": "For a circuit with n meshes: n simultaneous equations of the form [R][I] = [V] are set up and solved. In matrix form: R·I = V, where R is the resistance matrix, I is the column vector of mesh currents, and V is the column vector of source voltages."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "423bn8x",
      "content": "<strong>Node Analysis (Node Voltage Method)</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "av5opri",
      "content": "Node Analysis applies KCL at each node. Choose a reference node (ground, 0V). Assign node voltages V₁, V₂, … at other nodes. Write KCL at each node in terms of node voltages. Solve simultaneous equations. More efficient than mesh analysis when the number of nodes is less than the number of meshes."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "90m6iij",
      "content": "<strong>1.4  Star (Y) and Delta (Δ) Networks</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fr27zkm",
      "content": "Three-terminal resistive networks can be connected in Star (Y) or Delta (Δ) configuration. Conversion between the two is essential for solving complex three-phase circuits and bridge networks."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "znahs85",
      "content": "<strong>Delta to Star (Δ → Y) Conversion</strong>"
    },
    {
      "id": "w8wn0f1",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>R_A = (R_AB × R_CA) / (R_AB + R_BC + R_CA)</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>R_B = (R_AB × R_BC) / (R_AB + R_BC + R_CA)</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>R_C = (R_BC × R_CA) / (R_AB + R_BC + R_CA)</strong></p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "y653bdp",
      "content": "<strong>Star to Delta (Y → Δ) Conversion</strong>"
    },
    {
      "id": "whc1u6i",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>R_AB = R_A + R_B + (R_A·R_B)/R_C</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>R_BC = R_B + R_C + (R_B·R_C)/R_A</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>R_CA = R_C + R_A + (R_C·R_A)/R_B</strong></p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a295zvs",
      "content": "Special Case: When all three resistors in a delta are equal (R_Δ), each star resistor R_Y = R_Δ/3. Conversely, if all star resistors are equal R_Y, each delta resistor R_Δ = 3·R_Y."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wa5a07e",
      "content": "<strong>1.5  Practice MCQs — DC Circuits</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cwa2n9b",
      "content": "<strong>Q1.  </strong>Three resistors of 6Ω, 3Ω, and 2Ω are connected in parallel. What is the equivalent resistance?"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "cnrbdgi",
      "content": "        (A)  1 Ω"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "cqcd8tl",
      "content": "        (B)  11 Ω"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "3x4ib3f",
      "content": "        (C)  1.5 Ω"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "6qofezr",
      "content": "        (D)  3 Ω"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "nr452gr",
      "content": "<strong>Answer: </strong>(A)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hytua66",
      "content": "<strong>Q2.  </strong>A 12V battery is connected to a series circuit of 2Ω and 4Ω. What is the current in the circuit?"
